# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO WAYS TO REACH BATTLEGRID. Pick one.
#
# PERSONAL — your own account, your own key, on your own machine. Set
# BATTLEGRID_API_KEY below and skip the two OAuth variables entirely; they are
# not required when a key is set, because registering an OAuth client to talk to
# your own account is the ceremony this path exists to remove.
#
# DELEGATED — other people connect their accounts to your deployment. Register
# an OAuth client out of band and set the two variables below. Leave
# BATTLEGRID_API_KEY unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Registered out of band, once per environment, and pinned here.
# Registration is open and unauthenticated, so a client_id obtained at runtime
# would be unverifiable. Register with scope "mcp:read" only — wager authority
# is then unrequestable rather than merely unrequested.
BATTLEGRID_CLIENT_ID=

# Must exactly match a redirect_uri on the registration. No wildcards.
BATTLEGRID_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/battlegrid/callback

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNNING THE STACK ON DOCKER — `docker compose up -d`
#
# docker-compose.yml reads these three and builds DATABASE_URL from them itself.
# Do NOT also set DATABASE_URL for the containers: a container carrying two
# DATABASE_URL values takes the last one, and that is how the app once ran its
# schema check against one server and its runtime pool against another.
#
# The database publishes host port 5433, not 5432. A native PostgreSQL install
# commonly holds 5432, and with both listening `localhost:5432` resolves to
# whichever answers. 5432 means native; 5433 means this product.
POSTGRES_USER=gridcommander
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=grid_commander

# Only needed when running against a database yourself (`npm run dev`) rather
# than through the compose stack. Point it at 5433 if that stack is up.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/grid_commander

# NOTE: `npm run test:db` TRUNCATES every table it touches, including the signal
# record — which BattleGrid cannot re-serve, because it serves current readings
# only. Do not point it at the database above. Use a disposable one:
#
#   createdb grid_commander_test
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/grid_commander_test npm run db:migrate
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/grid_commander_test npm run test:db
#
# The suite refuses any database whose name does not say it is disposable. That
# guard exists because the suite was once run against a live database and
# destroyed a record that could not be rebuilt. Every test passed.

# 32 random bytes, base64. Tokens are encrypted at rest with this; a database
# dump alone must not be enough to use someone's BattleGrid account.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Signs the session cookie. Deliberately a different secret from the one above:
# this proves who you are, that one protects a BattleGrid token, and they have
# different blast radii when either leaks.
#
# Required. It is read on every request that resolves a session, so leaving it
# unset returns 500 on every page except /connect — which reads like a
# half-finished application rather than a missing variable. See
# env-example-missing-session-secret.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# Opt *out* of secure cookies, for local development over plain HTTP. Any value
# other than exactly "true" leaves them secure, so a typo fails safe rather than
# silently sending the session in the clear. Never set this in a deployment.
# ALLOW_INSECURE_COOKIES=true

# --- PERSONAL DEPLOYMENT ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Your own `bg_live_` key. Setting it means the product acts as you, and
# AUTHENTICATES NOBODY: anyone who can reach the page acts as you, reading your
# account and changing your agents. Correct on your own machine; a door with no
# lock anywhere else. Every page says so while it is set.
#
# With this set, BATTLEGRID_CLIENT_ID and BATTLEGRID_REDIRECT_URI are not needed.
# BATTLEGRID_API_KEY=

# What you say that key carries. Defaults to mcp:read.
#
# A DECLARATION, not a restriction. The product cannot read what a bg_live_ key
# actually holds, so this is what it will *attempt*, not what BattleGrid will
# allow. If your key can wager, declaring mcp:read does not stop the key — it
# stops this product from asking. What protects you is the classification guard
# and the confirmation gate, which is where the boundary always was.
#
# Space- or comma-separated. Valid values: mcp:read, mcp:wager.
# BATTLEGRID_KEY_SCOPES=mcp:read
